- How would you modify for multiple missing/duplicates?
- How would you verify correctness in edge cases, e.g., near-complete arrays?
- Could you design a purely arithmetic/XOR-based O(n) time, O(1) space variant and compare trade-offs?

## Why This Is Asked

Quicksort with many duplicates can degrade; 3-way partitioning reduces swaps and comparisons.

## Key Concepts

- 3-way partitioning (Dutch National Flag)
- In-place iterative sorting (no recursion)
- Median-of-three pivot to avoid degeneracy
- Space: O(log n) for manual stack

## Code Example

```javascript
function quicksort3WayIterative(arr) {
  const stack = [];
  stack.push([0, arr.length - 1]);
  while (stack.length) {
    const [l, r] = stack.pop();
    if (l >= r) continue;
    const m = l + Math.floor((r - l) / 2);
    // median-of-three to pick pivot
    if (arr[l] > arr[m]) [arr[l], arr[m]] = [arr[m], arr[l]];
    if (arr[l] > arr[r]) [arr[l], arr[r]] = [arr[r], arr[l]];
    if (arr[m] > arr[r]) [arr[m], arr[r]] = [arr[r], arr[m]];
    const pivot = arr[m];
    // Dutch National Flag partition
    let i = l, lt = l, gt = r;
    while (i <= gt) {
      if (arr[i] < pivot) { [arr[lt], arr[i]] = [arr[i], arr[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
      else if (arr[i] > pivot) { [arr[i], arr[gt]] = [arr[gt], arr[i]]; gt--; }
      else { i++; }
    }
    stack.push([l, lt - 1]);
    stack.push([gt + 1, r]);
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify stability with duplicates in this approach?
- How would you adapt to 64-bit integers and different memory constraints?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to blend topological ordering with DP to compute the longest-path in a weighted DAG and count the number of critical paths, including multiple sources and modulo arithmetic.

## Key Concepts
- Longest-path in DAG with node durations and edge setup weights
- Topological sort (Kahn) to process in dependency order
- DP for counting number of longest paths with modulo

## Code Example
```javascript
function countCriticalPaths(N, edges, dur) {
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  const incoming = Array.from({length: N}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(N).fill(0);

  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) {
    incoming[v].push([u, w]);
    indeg[v]++;
  }

  const EFT = dur.slice();
  const count = Array(N).fill(0);
  const q = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    if (indeg[i] === 0) {
      count[i] = 1;
      q.push(i);
    }
  }

  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    for (const [v, w] of incoming[u]) {
      const newEFT = EFT[u] + w + dur[v];
      if (newEFT > EFT[v]) {
        EFT[v] = newEFT;
        count[v] = count[u];
      } else if (newEFT === EFT[v]) {
        count[v] = (count[v] + count[u]) % MOD;
      }
      indeg[u]--;
      if (indeg[u] === 0) q.push(u);
    }
  }

  let maxEFT = 0, totalCritical = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    if (EFT[i] > maxEFT) {
      maxEFT = EFT[i];
      totalCritical = count[i];
    } else if (EFT[i] === maxEFT) {
      totalCritical = (totalCritical + count[i]) % MOD;
    }
  }
  return { EFT, maxEFT, criticalPaths: totalCritical };
}
```

## Complexity
- Time: O(N + M) for topological sort and DP
- Space: O(N + M) for adjacency and arrays
